e, but neutral pollsters still put Moody up 7-11 points"], "rationale": "Florida's Republican lean, Moody's massive fundraising advantage, and a Likely-to-Solid Republican ratings consensus outweigh signs of tightening from Democratic-leaning and local surveys; prediction markets price Nixon's chances near one in ten.", "watch": "If independent pollsters confirm the tied recent numbers rather than the seven-to-eleven-point Republican leads, or Nixon's Israel comments stop weighing on South Florida voters, the race could tighten further.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ashley Moody (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Appointed Senator Ashley Moody defends Florida's Senate seat against Democrat Angie Nixon in a special election. Moody carries the advantages of incumbency in a state that leans about five points Republican, and the money race is lopsided: she has raised $11.3 million to Nixon's roughly $1 million and holds more than $8 million on hand.\n\nPolling shows a competitive but Republican-favored contest. Neutral and Republican-leaning pollsters in September put Moody up by seven to eleven points, while Democratic-leaning and local surveys showed the race tied or nearly tied. Expert raters broadly call the race Likely or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Nixon roughly a ten percent chance. Nixon, a progressive, also faces questions about comments on Israel that could cost her support among South Florida Jewish voters. The seat remains the Republican's to lose.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1404, "margin": -5.31}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2259, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1936, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1439, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1034, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0984, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1233, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1499, "margin": -6.2}, {"date": "2026-